    "case_id": "549",
    "case_summary": "A 72-year-old man presented with poor appetite and fatigue for a week. The patient had a history of hypertension for years but no other chronic health problems, and did not take any medications regularly. No history of Raynaud phenomenon, swollen fingers, rashes, joint pain, coughing, or wheezing. Daily urine output decreased to less than 50 mL/d after admission.\n\nBlood pressure: 138/94 mm Hg. Bilateral lower limb edema present. No skin damage to the face, no swelling in the fingers in either hand, and no joint deformation or pain noted.\n\n- Laboratory Tests:    * Blood Tests: Leukocytes 3.9 × 10¹²/L, Erythrocytes 2.9 × 10¹²/L, Hemoglobin 87 × 10⁹/L, Platelets 43 × 10⁹/L    * Serum Tests: Creatinine 869 μmol/L, Urea 32 mmol/L, Albumin 31.4 g/L    * Immunological Tests: ANA positive (1:100), Anti-SM positive, U1-NRNP positive, Complement C3 0.07 g/L, C4 0.16 g/L    * Urine Analysis: Occult blood 3+ (dysmorphic), Protein 1+, Leukocytes 12/HPF, Casts negative- Imaging Studies:    * Kidney ultrasound: Enhanced echogenicity of both renal parenchyma    * Chest CT: Small amount of pleural effusion- Kidney Biopsy Findings:    * Light Microscopy: Large number of cellular crescents    * Immunofluorescence: Large amount of IgA deposition in mesangial area    * Electron Microscopy: Electron-dense deposits in mesangial areaFigure 1 Details:    * A: PAS staining showing cellular crescents    * B: Hematoxylin-eosin staining showing mild-to-moderate hyperplasia of mesangial cells and stroma    * C: Immunofluorescence showing IgA (3+) mass deposition along mesangium    * D: Electron microscopy showing electron-dense deposits in mesangium",
